From 83dc003d3cc8a672d4a74c374e79a39ee40b3c90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 02:55:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] script: HTML drag and drop within a document

DragEvent was undefined, HTMLElement.draggable was missing and pressing and
moving on a draggable element fired no drag events, so Trello-style boards,
sortable lists and drop zones built on native drag and drop did nothing.

This adds the DragEvent interface (also creatable through
document.createEvent), the draggable IDL attribute with its auto state for
images and links with href, and the drag-and-drop processing model in a new
DragAndDrop helper owned by DocumentEventHandler. An uncanceled primary
press on an element whose nearest draggable ancestor exists becomes a drag
once the pointer moves more than 4 CSS px, Chrome's threshold. dragstart
fires with a read/write drag data store (links and images pre-populate
text/uri-list, links also text/plain, as Chrome does); canceling it aborts
the drag. pointercancel then fires and pointer capture is released.

Each pointer move then fires drag at the source, and dragenter/dragleave
with related targets when the element under the pointer changes, otherwise
dragover, in the order Chrome's EventHandler::UpdateDragAndDrop uses.
Only a canceled dragover whose dropEffect is permitted by effectAllowed
makes the release fire drop; otherwise the release fires dragleave. dragend
always follows with the final operation as dropEffect. Every event gets a
fresh DataTransfer that borrows the store in the mode the spec gives that
event and is disassociated afterwards. While dragging, mouse and pointer
events, mouseup and click are suppressed and keys do not reach the page;
Escape cancels the drag, and leaving the viewport fires dragleave.

DataTransfer's effectAllowed setter wrote dropEffect; it now writes
effectAllowed, which dragstart handlers rely on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
